WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.099
Moving on to material optimizations. There are some really great

2
00:00:08.939 --> 00:00:14.240
optimizations you can put into your materials, some nodes which you may not be familiar with, so hopefully we can

3
00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:20.920
demonstrate something new today, but some material optimizations can give you a really good win in your project.

4
00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:26.640
So the first thing you want to be doing is building parent materials that then you create

5
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.159
material instances from. So we won't be going over how to create material instances in this course.

6
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.159
There are other courses on material instances, however

7
00:00:35.799 --> 00:00:40.719
just the concept of building a parent material that houses all the material instructions

8
00:00:41.039 --> 00:00:47.219
that will be your main base material and then from that material you can right-click in your content browser

9
00:00:47.759 --> 00:00:54.840
and convert and create a material instance from that parent material and therefore you can edit the individual

10
00:00:55.079 --> 00:01:01.500
parameters of that parent material without recompiling the entire material. So for example if you made a

11
00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:05.019
transparent glass material shader and then

12
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.700
you had a

13
00:01:07.719 --> 00:01:11.620
parameter on that parent material called glass color for example.

14
00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.099
You can then create multiple instances of that glass without

15
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:21.780
recompiling the parent material and you can change the glass color from green to a goldish

16
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:23.900
mustardy color

17
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.620
etc without

18
00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:28.019
recompiling. So for your material pipelines

19
00:01:28.019 --> 00:01:32.219
it's really good to get into the practice of having a parent material that houses

20
00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:35.459
all the instructions that you need and then just

21
00:01:35.819 --> 00:01:40.939
instancing off that parent material so that the majority of the materials in your map are actually

22
00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.099
instanced materials rather than the parent material.

23
00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.930
Moving on to some material switches

24
00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:52.969
you can get a static bool in your material if you'd like so you can actually

25
00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:59.849
decide to switch based on a given parameter here and then render the output so

26
00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:05.790
you can edit these switches at runtime so you can actually call in this instance

27
00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:13.889
be enable base color texture and if you enable that then it will turn green so maybe you might use this for an

28
00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:19.770
optimization pass so you want to set everything to just render this luminous green

29
00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:27.729
just to check what textures are being used where that might be a good use case for that. You can also get a static switch

30
00:02:27.969 --> 00:02:29.530
which

31
00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:36.050
has a similar similar effect so you know whether we're picking A or B we can still

32
00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:41.849
enable this at runtime if we would like another good useful switch there

33
00:02:41.849 --> 00:02:46.650
and then we can also get a mask switch so if you would just want to mask a certain channel

34
00:02:47.289 --> 00:02:52.129
from a mask that you might have packed with RG and B

35
00:02:52.849 --> 00:02:56.610
you can just mask based on a specific channel as well.

36
00:02:57.129 --> 00:03:05.020
We have some quality expression nodes, so we've got a shading path, so do you want to change the

37
00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:10.259
texture or whatever you might be inputting into this node

38
00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:17.300
dependent on the the rendering model that you're using so especially if you're trying to work across multiple

39
00:03:17.819 --> 00:03:22.659
platforms with maybe you've got deferred on by default and then you want to change it to a forward rendering

40
00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:26.819
maybe for a VR application or you want a mobile application

41
00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:32.300
you can change the shading path and therefore the instruction count of that material

42
00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:34.900
based on the renderer that you're using

43
00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:41.340
Again we can have a feature level switch so we could use these within the materials say you want these

44
00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:48.900
different switches inside your game to say okay, I want to go at this level of detail for example for

45
00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:53.419
SM5 and SM6. Okay, we'll set this and it's almost like

46
00:03:53.979 --> 00:04:00.580
when we're using those texture groups and we were saying okay batch all these into this setting you can actually

47
00:04:01.259 --> 00:04:02.979
decide on a

48
00:04:02.979 --> 00:04:05.340
larger scale in your materials which

49
00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:07.979
feature level you want to select

50
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.659
dependent on your texture quality you can say okay if we're running a

51
00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:14.860
super low

52
00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:22.819
texture quality or or even a high texture quality use this type of color if we're running on

53
00:04:23.379 --> 00:04:27.779
medium or epic for example use this type of texture sample so

54
00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:30.480
You know maybe the texture has been

55
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.500
imported a different

56
00:04:34.259 --> 00:04:37.180
resolution maybe you want to make some affordances for the

57
00:04:37.699 --> 00:04:42.019
Amount of information going in from a low quality switch to a higher quality switch

58
00:04:42.139 --> 00:04:45.620
Yeah that that quality node is available for you there

59
00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:52.259
And then also you you have a ray tracing quality switch, so if if you want to support

60
00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:58.060
Real ray traced materials and non ray traced materials in the same project

61
00:04:58.139 --> 00:05:04.220
But you want to make some affordances for someone that might not have ray tracing enabled then you can

